    "text": "Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>'</mo></msup><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> be two circles. If the set of all values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>λ</mi></math> so that the circles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mo>'</mo></math> intersect at two distinct points, is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, then the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> lies on the curve:",
